# Standard library: reasoning
#
# The laws of logic a proof cites by name: excluded middle, double negation,
# and the rest of what takes a proof from some claims to another.
#
# One record per item. A record begins at column 0 with its kind and name; a
# proof cites it as def:stdlib/reasoning/<name> or thm:stdlib/reasoning/<name>,
# and the prefix must match the kind. Format is described in DATABASE.md.
#
# An item's statement is written in the theorem form of SYNTAX.md: let and
# assume lines with labels, then a `then` line.
#
# Every item carries at least one of:
#   metamath    a set.mm label or labels supply it
#   open        it is cited but unproved and unbridged
#
# Other fields: target, first-used, note.


theorem excluded-middle
  then        P or not P
  metamath    exmid
  target      exmid
  first-used  cantor
  note        The first citation to substitute a formula for a variable rather
              than a term. A school reader has never been told that "P or not P"
              is a law, which is why it is cited and not folded into a method.

theorem from-contradiction
  assume P                                                            (H1)
  assume not P                                                        (H2)
  then        Q
  metamath    pm2.21
  target      pm2.21
  first-used  intermediate-value
  note        Closes an impossible case of a `cases` block so that every case
              ends in the block's common claim.

theorem double-negation
  assume not not P                                                    (H1)
  then        P
  metamath    notnotr
  target      notnotr
  first-used  cantor
  note        The step intuitionistic logic refuses, so citing it is the text
              saying which logic it is in. It appears because the contradiction
              rule demands the literal negation of the claim, and a claim that
              is itself a negation therefore produces a doubled one.

theorem disjunctive-syllogism
  assume P or Q                                                       (H1)
  assume not Q                                                        (H2)
  then        P
  metamath    orel2
  target      orel2
  first-used  sqrt2-irrational
  note        The √2 pilot asked whether a school reader can check this on
              paper, and called it the acceptance test. Citing it puts the
              question where a reader can see it rather than inside a method.
